            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Dew primarily symbolizes divine blessing, favor, life-giving refreshment, and grace, with secondary usages representing transience or God's teaching.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Dew symbolizes the word of God descending from heaven, which provides daily bread but can evaporate if received without root.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Divine blessing, favor, and refreshment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The word of God.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book rejects the primary consensus view of dew as a general symbol for blessing, grace, or the Holy Spirit, restricting its meaning exclusively to the Word of God.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus views dew's primary referent as God's general favor, provision, or spiritual refreshment. The book identifies dew specifically as the Word of God, uniquely synthesizing the biblical motif of dew's transience with the parabolic imagery of the word being received without root.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book elevates a recognized secondary consensus alternative (the Word, based on Deut 32:2) to the exclusive primary meaning, fundamentally changing the core referent from general blessing or the Holy Spirit to the spoken/written Word of God.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In passages like Hosea 14:5 ('I will be as the dew unto Israel'), God compares Himself directly to the dew, and in Isaac's blessing (Gen 27:28), it represents agricultural prosperity. The consensus's broader category of 'divine blessing or presence' fits these contexts much better than strictly limiting dew to 'the word.'\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
